text:
sam-eng: quick question — for the JS SDK should we prefer global fetch (when present) or ship undici as an explicit dependency? thinking about ESM/CJS interop + edge runtimes

 tia-p: my vote is: prefer globalThis.fetch, fallback to undici in Node, avoid bundling polyfills into edge bundles. smaller bundles for edge apps is critical.

 ronnie-s: agreed. also watch out for typings — if we rely on global fetch consumers need DOM lib in tsconfig or our types need to reference the undici types.

 maria-w: edge runtimes (Vercel Edge, Cloudflare Workers, Deno, Bun) all have different fetch semantics. We should document the supported runtimes and provide a tiny runtime-detection snippet in the README.

 sam-eng: rough sketch I shared in thread:

 ```js
 // runtime-fetch.js (ESM-friendly)
 export async function runtimeFetch() {
   if (typeof globalThis.fetch === 'function') return globalThis.fetch;
   // dynamic import so bundlers can tree-shake away when not needed
   try {
     const { fetch } = await import('undici');
     return fetch;
   } catch (err) {
     throw new Error('No fetch available. Please polyfill or install undici.');
   }
 }
 ```

 tia-p: that looks good. caveat: top-level await — consumers using CommonJS might need a sync path. we could expose a sync getter that returns undefined if not available and force async init in SDK startup.

 ronnie-s: for CJS compatibility we can export a shim: `getFetchSync()` that checks globalThis.fetch synchronously and otherwise returns null. then internal SDK init can `await ensureFetch()` which dynamic-imports undici if needed. keeps require() consumers working.

 maria-w: about typings: propose we ship our public API types using the minimal Fetch types (RequestInfo, RequestInit, Response) by copying small subset to our types file with a comment, to avoid forcing \"lib\": [\"DOM\"] for Node consumers. or we can add a devDependency on @types/node-fetch? either way, document the tradeoffs.

 sam-eng: prefer minimal in-repo types (small) + a clear note. example in types.ts: `export type Fetch = (input: RequestInfo, init?: RequestInit) => Promise<Response>` and include our own RequestInfo/RequestInit aliases. keeps consumers happier.

 tia-p: retry/auth behavior note — undici has connection pooling options; global fetch does not. Our SDK should implement retries at higher level (idempotency on GET/HEAD, exponential backoff) and token refresh hooks so we don't rely on underlying fetch for that.

 sam-eng: action items:
 - create PR with runtime-fetch.js + CJS shim (sam)
 - add short doc in sdk/README about runtime compatibility and required polyfills (maria)
 - add tiny types file to avoid forcing dom lib (ronnie)
 - implement high-level retry wrapper and token-refresh hook (tia)

 maria-w: small UX ask: include an example for Next.js edge and a note for Next API routes (server vs edge). many users are confused which polyfills get included by Next.

 devex-bot: reminder: please add tests covering Node 16/18/20, Bun, and Cloudflare Workers compatibility matrices.

 sam-eng: noted. I can put a draft in `sdk/runtimes.md` and start the PR later today. if everyone OK I'll target not bundling undici into the edge build and instead rely on the dynamic import fallback for Node-only installs.

 ronnie-s: fine by me. one more: package.json docs + example snippet for pnpm/webpack users who might accidentally polyfill fetch globally and break edge behavior.

 tia-p: merging policy: default to global fetch; SDK auto-fallback to undici in Node; we document when users must `npm install undici` (or we mark it optionalPeerDependency?). prefer optionalDependency so we don't force install for edge-only users.

 sam-eng: good call. I'll draft the PR with optionalDependency and the detection shim.

 maria-w: sweet — thanks.

 sam-eng: thanks all. will ping when I open the PR with links to the runtime notes.
